WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:02.367
-Ooh, you look good.
I love this.

00:00:02.367 --> 00:00:03.501
-Hey, you like that?
-The mustache, yes!

00:00:03.501 --> 00:00:06.000
-This is for the play.
-Wow.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.067
-But we darken it a little bit
for the play.

00:00:08.067 --> 00:00:09.734
-You colored the --
You colored the 'stache?

00:00:09.734 --> 00:00:12.901
-I color it for the play.
Now it's kind of -- it's ginger.

00:00:12.901 --> 00:00:15.033
I'm a little ginger there.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:15.033 --> 00:00:16.767
-Yeah, yeah, I like it.
-You know, for theater,

00:00:16.767 --> 00:00:19.400
you want it to kind of pop.
-Because you are more of, like,

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:21.501
a brown, I would say,
like a brown-haired man.

00:00:21.501 --> 00:00:23.834
-Yeah, kind of brown,
yeah, light brown.

00:00:23.834 --> 00:00:25.601
But then, the facial hair
is kind of...

00:00:25.601 --> 00:00:26.734
-More ginger.
-...ginger, yeah.

00:00:26.734 --> 00:00:28.267
-Yeah.
-I always wanted to be, like,

00:00:28.267 --> 00:00:30.167
Italian as a young actor,
so I would --

00:00:30.167 --> 00:00:31.467
You know, Robert de Niro,
whatever.

00:00:31.467 --> 00:00:33.701
Won't be in a Scorsese movie.
So I'd darken it in.

00:00:33.701 --> 00:00:36.467
For auditions,
I'd get newspaper print

00:00:36.467 --> 00:00:39.300
and put it on my face
to get that sort of Don Johnson,

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:41.400
swarthy, kind of...
-Is that real?

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:42.634
-Yeah.
-And did it ever --

00:00:42.634 --> 00:00:44.701
-It worked, yeah.
It makes you look kind of like,

00:00:44.701 --> 00:00:46.267
you know, rugged.
-Five o'clock shadow.

00:00:46.267 --> 00:00:48.033
-Yeah.
-I would totally do that.

00:00:48.033 --> 00:00:49.100
[ Laughter ]

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:50.534
-It works.
-And you got the roles?

00:00:50.534 --> 00:00:52.834
-And now we have make-up
to do that.

00:00:52.834 --> 00:00:54.234
-Yes.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:54.234 --> 00:00:57.901
-But rubbing newspaper on it
is actually -- I enjoy that.

00:00:57.901 --> 00:00:59.567
-A little trick.
A little trick.

00:00:59.567 --> 00:01:01.567
-I want to talk about
"American Buffalo."

00:01:01.567 --> 00:01:04.300
-Yeah, yeah, yeah, man.
Thanks for having me.

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:05.300
-Because this is --
Oh, come on.

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:06.534
You know we love you, man.

00:01:06.534 --> 00:01:08.267
I don't know what I'd do
without Sam Rockwell.

00:01:08.267 --> 00:01:10.367
Man, I love you buddy, come on.

00:01:10.367 --> 00:01:13.901
But this play was supposed
to be -- is it two years ago?

00:01:13.901 --> 00:01:15.267
-This is supposed to be
two years ago.

00:01:15.267 --> 00:01:18.767
Were shut down at the very
beginning of lockdown, pandemic.

00:01:18.767 --> 00:01:20.534
-Is that tough?
-It was very tough.

00:01:20.534 --> 00:01:22.267
It was kind of heartbreaking.
Of course, we thought

00:01:22.267 --> 00:01:24.334
it would be over
in two weeks, a month.

00:01:24.334 --> 00:01:26.501
And so Fishburne and I
continued to run lines

00:01:26.501 --> 00:01:28.767
on FaceTime and Zooms,
and occasionally

00:01:28.767 --> 00:01:30.267
Neil, the director, and Darren
would join.

00:01:30.267 --> 00:01:33.767
And we just did it because
we were losing our minds.

00:01:33.767 --> 00:01:35.100
-Yeah, "This will be over
in a month.

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:37.567
This will be over."
-Yeah, and sometimes,

00:01:37.567 --> 00:01:40.334
even three times a week,
we'd run lines.

00:01:40.334 --> 00:01:43.567
But, also, just to keep
our sanity, I think.

00:01:43.567 --> 00:01:45.434
-Just to entertain yourself.
-Exactly, yeah.

00:01:45.434 --> 00:01:48.033
-During the lockdown, yeah.
-During the lockdown.

00:01:48.033 --> 00:01:49.267
-Can you set up
what the play is about?

00:01:49.267 --> 00:01:51.734
It take place in a pawnshop.

00:01:51.734 --> 00:01:54.667
-It's in a pawnshop.
All these guys are either

00:01:54.667 --> 00:01:58.000
thieves, junkies,
or ex-convicts, and they're --

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:02.534
they're all trying to get this
American Buffalo nickel,

00:02:02.534 --> 00:02:07.200
with also maybe hopes of having
a lot of other valuable nickels

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:08.734
to make a lot of money.
-Yeah.

00:02:08.734 --> 00:02:10.667
-That's what the play is about.

00:02:10.667 --> 00:02:14.033
It's kind of like "Seinfeld"...
[ Laughter ]

00:02:14.033 --> 00:02:17.067
...but stealing.
-Yeah, yeah, okay, yeah.

00:02:17.067 --> 00:02:19.501
But this is --
Your character, Teach...

00:02:19.501 --> 00:02:21.434
-Yes, yes.
-Teach is the character.

00:02:21.434 --> 00:02:26.234
Before this had been played
by Al Pacino, Robert Duvall,

00:02:26.234 --> 00:02:28.968
Dustin Hoffman.
-That's right.

00:02:28.968 --> 00:02:30.334
Did they have a mustache?
I don't think so.

00:02:30.334 --> 00:02:31.534
-I don't think so.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:31.534 --> 00:02:33.501
I don't think so.
[ Both laugh ]

00:02:33.501 --> 00:02:35.200
Would you get nervous
if any of them came?

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:37.534
-Oh, yeah, I would be
very nervous, yeah.

00:02:37.534 --> 00:02:41.033
I did "Fool for Love,"
and Ed Harris originated

00:02:41.033 --> 00:02:42.868
that role,
and I would have nightmares

00:02:42.868 --> 00:02:45.067
because I used to do
the lasso with that

00:02:45.067 --> 00:02:47.901
and nail objects with a --
And I had nightmares

00:02:47.901 --> 00:02:51.033
that Ed Harris would see me
lasso really badly.

00:02:51.033 --> 00:02:55.767
And actually, he came,
and it went pretty well.

00:02:55.767 --> 00:02:57.767
I lassoed good that night.
Thank God.

00:02:57.767 --> 00:03:00.667
-He didn't stand up
and point at you and laugh?

00:03:00.667 --> 00:03:02.968
[ Laughter ]

00:03:02.968 --> 00:03:04.367
-In my dreams.
-Yeah.

00:03:04.367 --> 00:03:05.767
-In my nightmares he did.
-He would do that.

00:03:05.767 --> 00:03:07.300
-Yeah.
-But in real life, no.

00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:09.200
Ed Harris, pretty good guy.
-He was a good guy.

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:10.801
No, it was great.

00:03:10.801 --> 00:03:14.100
-I've seen you on Broadway
and act.

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:15.934
-You've seen me on the Broadway.
-I've seen you on the Broadway.

00:03:15.934 --> 00:03:18.167
-Yes, yes.
-But does it bum you out

00:03:18.167 --> 00:03:19.968
when you have friends
in the crowd?

00:03:19.968 --> 00:03:22.300
-I don't like to know
when people are out there.

00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:23.434
-Really?

00:03:23.434 --> 00:03:25.601
-I think Fishburne
and Darren don't mind.

00:03:25.601 --> 00:03:28.200
-Because why? Do you try
to put too much mustard on it?

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:30.200
-Yeah, exactly, exactly.

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:32.734
-You try to show me your chops
too much, I go,

00:03:32.734 --> 00:03:34.501
"Sam, you're good, buddy."
-"Hey, calm down."

00:03:34.501 --> 00:03:36.234
-You got the Oscar, bud,
we're good.

00:03:36.234 --> 00:03:38.100
I know how good you are.
You don't have to teach me.

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:40.033
-Dude, relax.
-Jimmy Fallon, man, that's good.

00:03:40.033 --> 00:03:41.501
And you're like,
"Jimmy, sit down."

00:03:41.501 --> 00:03:44.667
You're not bothering me.
It's Ed Harris is bothering me.

00:03:44.667 --> 00:03:46.667
I don't care if you're here,
buddy."

00:03:46.667 --> 00:03:48.434
But I don't know --
-No, that would make me nervous

00:03:48.434 --> 00:03:49.968
if you were there,
you and Nancy.

00:03:49.968 --> 00:03:53.601
-I don't know if I would
come back to see you.

00:03:53.601 --> 00:03:55.033
Is that rude?

00:03:55.033 --> 00:03:56.934
-No. You know what?
That's actually not rude.

00:03:56.934 --> 00:04:00.767
I think it's actually good.
Let the actors, you know --

00:04:00.767 --> 00:04:01.934
-Wonder.
-Yeah.

00:04:01.934 --> 00:04:03.734
[ Laughter ]

00:04:03.734 --> 00:04:04.901
-If I enjoyed it.

00:04:04.901 --> 00:04:07.734
-But also, just, like,
just let them go to bed.

00:04:07.734 --> 00:04:09.434
You know, you don't have to --
-That's what I'm saying.

00:04:09.434 --> 00:04:11.701
-I just go --
you just performed for me,

00:04:11.701 --> 00:04:12.868
amazing for an hour and a half.

00:04:12.868 --> 00:04:15.300
-Then you got to do like
a backstage talk show.

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:18.534
-Yeah. "Hey, do you mind, Sam,
can I get a selfie?"

00:04:18.534 --> 00:04:20.534
And you go, "Yeah, Jimmy,
I can, but I just

00:04:20.534 --> 00:04:22.133
entertained you
for two hours, bud."

00:04:22.133 --> 00:04:23.300
But I feel bad.

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:25.100
-You want to go to sleep
after you do this, right?

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:26.834
You want to go to bed.
-Yes.

00:04:26.834 --> 00:04:28.434
-Yeah.
-And you know it so well,

00:04:28.434 --> 00:04:31.501
you're like, "I'm in the zone"?

00:04:31.501 --> 00:04:33.767
-I never feel on cruise control
with this thing.

00:04:33.767 --> 00:04:36.300
I always feel like, "Oh, you
better stay on the tracks."

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:37.501
-Really?
-Yeah.

00:04:37.501 --> 00:04:39.868
-Because you're in the circle
in the square.

00:04:39.868 --> 00:04:42.267
-Yeah.
-Which is --

00:04:42.267 --> 00:04:44.200
The audience is around you.
-Yeah.

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:45.767
It's so easy to fall off
the train tracks.

00:04:45.767 --> 00:04:48.000
As you know with "SNL"
and stuff --

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:49.868
-You got to be focused.
-You got to be focused live.

00:04:49.868 --> 00:04:51.767
-Yeah, no,
I never broke character.

00:04:51.767 --> 00:04:52.934
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:04:52.934 --> 00:04:55.234
-I was --
-You never corpsed?

00:04:55.234 --> 00:04:56.868
-No, I never corpsed.
-No, you never corpsed.

00:04:56.868 --> 00:04:58.567
-But, see, I would be --

00:04:58.567 --> 00:04:59.968
'Cause the crowd's
right up on you there,

00:04:59.968 --> 00:05:02.000
I would be distracted by --
because because I've been

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:06.234
to many a play and someone
always next to me

00:05:06.234 --> 00:05:09.400
with the coughing --
like a decent phlegmy cough.

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:11.334
-Totally.
-Wherever I go, whatever night,

00:05:11.334 --> 00:05:15.300
they're like --
[ Imitating cough ]

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:16.767
I go, "Wow!"

00:05:16.767 --> 00:05:18.067
-You think they have
tuberculosis.

00:05:18.067 --> 00:05:21.100
-I want them to go on stage.
Show me the dying scene.

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:23.234
[ Laughter ]

00:05:23.234 --> 00:05:26.067
[ Imitating cough ]

00:05:26.067 --> 00:05:28.000
-Thank God they have masks now.
-I know.

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:30.033
-It makes sense.
There was a guy sleeping

00:05:30.033 --> 00:05:31.834
in the front row the other day.

00:05:31.834 --> 00:05:33.701
I said to Fishburne,
"You see that, Laurence?

00:05:33.701 --> 00:05:36.501
The guy was out there sleeping
in the front row."

00:05:36.501 --> 00:05:39.133
-Isn't that unbelievable?
-[ Snores ]

00:05:39.133 --> 00:05:41.501
-It's dark, it's warm.
-Yeah.

00:05:41.501 --> 00:05:43.801
-Right? And you're like,
"I'm in a good spot right now."

00:05:43.801 --> 00:05:46.968
-In the front row!
The balls on the guy.

00:05:46.968 --> 00:05:49.133
-What in the world?
This guy got tickets

00:05:49.133 --> 00:05:51.667
to go see great actors --
-I got pretty loud, though.

00:05:51.667 --> 00:05:52.868
I tried to wake him up.
I got a little loud.

00:05:52.868 --> 00:05:53.934
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:05:53.934 --> 00:05:55.334
That's what I would do, too.

00:05:55.334 --> 00:05:57.467
Also project a little bit.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:57.467 --> 00:05:58.901
-Yeah.
-You know what I'm saying?

00:05:58.901 --> 00:06:00.634
-Spit, project.
-Yeah, a little spit take.

00:06:00.634 --> 00:06:01.767
"What?"
[ Spits ]

00:06:01.767 --> 00:06:04.367
-A little anger.
-"Yeah, sorry to wake you up

00:06:04.367 --> 00:06:05.968
from your nap there, buddy.
Gosh."

00:06:05.968 --> 00:06:07.567
-We're just
doing a little show here.

00:06:07.567 --> 00:06:08.601
-"Not a big deal, yeah.

00:06:08.601 --> 00:06:10.167
We just worked
two years on this!

00:06:10.167 --> 00:06:13.167
Sorry, buddy, yeah."
[ Laughter ]

00:06:13.167 --> 00:06:15.834
"American Buffalo"
right now on Broadway.

00:06:15.834 --> 00:06:17.100
I want to talk about
"The Bad Guys"

00:06:17.100 --> 00:06:18.300
more after the break.
Come on back.

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:19.968
More with Sam Rockwell,
everybody.

